<div class="page-hero">
  <div class="page-eyebrow">Transparency first</div>
  <h1>How FitMyPack Score™ Works</h1>
  <p>A transparent look at the algorithm behind the scores — and why torso length is the most important measurement for backpack fit.</p>
</div>

<div class="content">

  <div class="section">
    <h2 class="section-label-h2">The problem</h2>
    <h3>Why most hikers buy the wrong size pack</h3>
    <p>Most backpack size guides tell you to pick a size based on your height. But height alone is a poor predictor of pack fit. Two people of identical height can have very different torso lengths — and it's the torso that determines how a pack actually carries weight.</p>
    <p>A pack that doesn't match your torso length will transfer weight to your shoulders instead of your hips. This causes pain, fatigue and poor balance on the trail — even on an expensive, well-designed pack.</p>
    <div class="highlight-box">
      The single most important measurement for backpack fit is <strong>torso length</strong> — not height, not body weight, not shoulder width.
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section">
    <h2 class="section-label-h2">The measurements</h2>
    <h3>How to measure your torso and waist</h3>
    <p>You need two measurements. Both take under two minutes with a soft tape measure.</p>
    <div style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 160px;gap:24px;align-items:start;">
    <div class="measure-steps">
      <div class="measure-step">
        <span class="step-num">01</span>
        <div class="step-body">
          <strong>Torso length — find your C7 vertebra.</strong> Tilt your head forward. Feel the base of your neck — the most prominent bony bump is C7. This is your top measurement point.
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="measure-step">
        <span class="step-num">02</span>
        <div class="step-body">
          <strong>Find your iliac crest.</strong> Place your hands on your hips with thumbs pointing backwards. The top edge of your hip bones is the iliac crest. This is your bottom measurement point.
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="measure-step">
        <span class="step-num">03</span>
        <div class="step-body">
          <strong>Measure.</strong> Run a soft tape measure from C7 down your spine to the iliac crest. Record this distance in <strong>inches or centimetres</strong> — FitMyPack accepts both.
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="measure-step">
        <span class="step-num">04</span>
        <div class="step-body">
          <strong>Waist circumference.</strong> Measure around your torso at the level of your belly button — this is approximately where the pack's hip belt will sit. Record this in <strong>inches or centimetres</strong>.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section">
    <h2 class="section-label-h2">The algorithm</h2>
    <h3>How the FitMyPack Score™ is calculated</h3>
    <p>Every <a href="/backpacks/" style="color:var(--accent);text-decoration:underline;">hiking backpack in our database</a> has manufacturer-verified fit ranges: a minimum and maximum torso length, and a minimum and maximum waist circumference for each size. When you enter your measurements, we run this calculation for every pack and every size:</p>

    <div class="formula-box">
      <div class="formula-title">FitMyPack Score™ — v1 formula</div>
      <div class="formula-line">If torso OR waist is <span>outside the manufacturer range</span> → Score = <span>0 (Not Compatible)</span></div>
      <div class="formula-line">If both measurements are <span>inside the range</span>:</div>
      <div class="formula-line">&nbsp;&nbsp;Torso score = 100 − (distance from centre of range ÷ half-range × <span>20</span>)</div>
      <div class="formula-line">&nbsp;&nbsp;Waist score = 100 − (distance from centre of range ÷ half-range × <span>15</span>)</div>
      <div class="formula-line">&nbsp;&nbsp;<span>Final score</span> = Torso score × 0.70 + Waist score × 0.30</div>
    </div>

    <p>The torso score carries 70% of the weight because torso length is the primary determinant of how a pack carries. The waist measurement carries 30% — it determines hip belt fit, which affects load transfer.</p>
    <p>Scores closer to the centre of the manufacturer's range score higher. A measurement at the very edge of the range will score around 80 — the pack will fit, but with less adjustment range available.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="section">
    <h2 class="section-label-h2">Score interpretation</h2>
    <h3>What each score means</h3>
    <table class="score-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Score</th>
          <th>Label</th>
          <th>What it means</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>90–100</td>
          <td><span class="badge excellent">Excellent Fit</span></td>
          <td>Your measurements fall near the centre of this size's range. Maximum adjustment available, optimal load transfer.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>75–89</td>
          <td><span class="badge good">Good Fit</span></td>
          <td>Your measurements fit within range but closer to the edges. The pack will fit well with proper adjustment.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>60–74</td>
          <td><span class="badge fair">Fair Fit</span></td>
          <td>At the limits of the manufacturer's stated range. May fit with maximum strap adjustment.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>0</td>
          <td><span class="badge nc">Not Compatible</span></td>
          <td>Your torso or waist is outside the manufacturer's stated range. This size is not recommended for your body.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="section">
    <h2 class="section-label-h2">Multi-size packs</h2>
    <h3>How we handle packs with multiple sizes</h3>
    <p>Most serious hiking packs come in multiple sizes (S/M, L/XL or Small/Medium/Large). In the backpack listing, we calculate the FitMyPack Score™ for every available size and display the <strong>best score</strong> on the card.</p>
    <p>On the individual pack page, you can see the score breakdown for each size — so you know exactly which size to order.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="section">
    <h2 class="section-label-h2">Our database</h2>
    <h3>How we build and verify our data</h3>
    <p>Every fit specification in our database is sourced directly from manufacturer product pages — we do not estimate, interpolate or invent data. If a manufacturer does not publish torso or waist fit data for a size, that size is excluded from scoring.</p>
    <p>Measurements listed in inches are converted to centimetres (× 2.54). Weights in pounds are converted to kilograms (÷ 2.205). All conversions are exact.</p>
    <p>FitMyPack was built by a team of outdoor enthusiasts frustrated by the lack of body-specific backpack comparison tools. We have no commercial relationship with any brand in our database.</p>
  </div>

